<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
  <question type="aitext">
    <exampledescription>Interpret a chest X-ray showing a pneumothorax: diagnosis, key finding and next step. The image is described to the AI in the prompt.</exampledescription>
    <name>
      <text>Chest X-ray interpretation: pneumothorax</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/right_pneumothorax_cxr.svg" alt="PA chest radiograph" width="660" height="512"></p>
<p>Examine the chest X-ray above. Identify the primary acute abnormality present, describe the key visual finding that led to your diagnosis, and state the immediate next step in management.</p>]]></text>
      <file name="right_pneumothorax_cxr.svg" path="/" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The image shows a right-sided pneumothorax. A thin visceral pleural line is visible with no lung markings (vessels) lateral to it. Immediate management is a chest drain (tube thoracostomy), or needle decompression if there are signs of tension, with urgent respiratory referral.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>10</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber>med-chest-xray-pneumothorax</idnumber>
    <responseformat>plain</responseformat>
    <responsefieldlines>6</responsefieldlines>
    <minwordlimit/>
    <maxwordlimit>200</maxwordlimit>
    <graderinfo format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Diagnosis: right pneumothorax. Visual evidence: visceral pleural line / absent peripheral lung markings. Management: chest drain or needle decompression (or urgent respiratory referral).</p>]]></text>
    </graderinfo>
    <responsetemplate format="html">
      <text/>
    </responsetemplate>
    <aiprompt>You cannot see the image the student was shown, so use this description of it: a PA erect chest radiograph showing a right-sided pneumothorax. The right lung is collapsed towards the hilum, a thin white visceral pleural line is visible, and lateral to that line the right hemithorax is uniformly dark with no lung or vascular markings. The left lung, heart and diaphragm are normal.

Assess the student's answer based on three key components:
Diagnosis: They must state pneumothorax (or collapsed lung).
Visual evidence: They must mention observing a visible pleural line OR absence of lung markings/vessels in the peripheral lung field.
Management: They must mention chest drain / tube thoracostomy or needle decompression (or urgent respiratory referral).
Provide constructive feedback to the student explaining what they got right or missed.</aiprompt>
    <markscheme>10 marks: Correctly identifies pneumothorax, correctly describes the visual finding (pleural line or absent lung markings), AND states chest drain / needle decompression.
7 marks: Correctly diagnoses pneumothorax and notes the visual finding, but omits or gives an incomplete management step.
4 marks: Correctly names pneumothorax only, without visual justification or management details.
0 marks: Incorrect diagnosis (e.g. calling it pneumonia, pleural effusion, or a normal X-ray).</markscheme>
    <model/>
    <spellcheck>0</spellcheck>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>The X-ray shows a right-sided pneumothorax. You can see a distinct visceral pleural edge with no lung vascular markings past it. The patient needs a chest drain.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>This is a pneumothorax because the right side has a clear line where the lung stopped expanding.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>The lung looks consolidation-heavy, suggesting lobar pneumonia. Recommend antibiotics.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
